{red|**Begin assessing your financial situation.**

What is your approximate monthly income from all sources?}{red| What are your major monthly expenses?}{red| Do you have savings?}{red| Approximately how much?}{red| Do you have debt?}{red| Approximately how much?}{red| Are you currently gaining ground, losing ground, or breaking even each month?}

Write what you know about your financial picture:

{red|**Explore the emotional side of your financial situation.**

What emotions come up when you think about money?}{red| How is financial stress affecting your daily life?}{red| What fears about money keep you up at night?}{red| How have emotions affected your financial decisions?}

Write about your financial emotions:

{red|**The goal is income equal to or greater than expenses.** If expenses exceed income, something must change.

**First, look for expenses to cut.** What is truly necessary versus nice to have?}{red| Subscriptions you do not use?}{red| Spending that could be reduced?}

{red|**Second, look for income to increase.** Overtime?}{red| Side work?}{red| Selling items you no longer need?}{red| Better paying job?}

**Third, prioritize ruthlessly.** If you cannot cover everything, decide what matters most. Housing, utilities, food, and transportation come before entertainment.

**The goal is not perfection but sustainability.** A budget that you can actually follow that keeps you stable.

{red|**Create your debt elimination plan.**

Which strategy will you use: snowball (smallest first) or avalanche (highest interest first)?}

{red|What order will you pay off your debts?}

{red|How much extra can you put toward debt each month beyond minimums?}

{red|Where will that extra come from?}

{red|What is your target date for being debt-free?}

Write your debt elimination plan:

{red|**Assess your current savings situation.**

How much do you currently have in emergency savings?}{red| How close is that to your starter goal of five hundred to one thousand dollars?}{red| How close to your ultimate goal of three to six months expenses?}{red| What has prevented you from saving in the past?}

Write about your savings status:

{red|**Create a plan to build your emergency fund.**

What is your starter goal?}{red| Usually five hundred to one thousand dollars.

How much can you save each paycheck or month toward this goal?}

{red|When will you set up automatic transfers?}

{red|What additional sources could contribute: windfalls, selling items, reduced expenses?}

{red|When do you expect to reach your starter goal?}

Write your emergency fund plan:

{red|**Several options exist for increasing income.**

**Advance in your current job.** Could you take on more responsibility, get a raise, or pursue a promotion?}

{red|**Find a better paying job.** Is your current job paying market rate?}{red| Could you find something better?}

{red|**Add a part-time job.** Temporary second work can accelerate financial goals.

**Develop a side hustle.** Freelancing, consulting, or gig work using your skills.

**Monetize hobbies.** Could something you enjoy doing generate income?}

**Sell items.** Things you no longer need can become cash.

**Pursue education or training.** Sometimes investing in yourself leads to higher earning potential.

**Not all options are available to everyone.** Consider what might work for your situation.

{red|**Explore your options for increasing income.**

Is there potential to earn more in your current job?}{red| Would a job change improve your income?}{red| What skills do you have that could generate additional income?}{red| Do you have time and energy for additional work?}{red| How much?}{red| What constraints limit your options?}

Write about your income options:

{red|**If increasing income would help your situation, create a plan.**

What is one concrete step you could take toward increasing income?}

{red|What timeline makes sense for this step?}

{red|What barriers might you face and how could you address them?}

**If increasing income is not viable right now, note that and focus energy on managing what you have.**

Write your income growth plan or decision:

{red|**Assess your current financial knowledge and confidence.**

What aspects of financial management do you feel confident about?}{red| What areas feel overwhelming or confusing?}{red| What financial tasks have you never done that you now need to do?}{red| What would help you feel more confident?}

Write about your financial confidence:

{red|**Create a plan to build your financial knowledge and confidence.**

What is one financial skill you want to develop first?}

{red|What resource will you use to learn it?}

{red|What is one financial task you have been avoiding that you will tackle this week?}

{red|Who could you ask for help or guidance?}

Write your financial learning plan:

{red|**Reflect on stewardship and giving in your current situation.**

How do you view your relationship with money and God?}{red| Have you been able to give?}{red| How has that felt?}{red| What tensions do you feel between tight finances and biblical generosity?}{red| What would faithful stewardship look like for you in this season?}

Write your stewardship reflection:

{red|**Make a stewardship commitment appropriate for your situation.**

How will you honor God with your finances in this season?}

{red|What will giving look like for you, even if small?}

{red|How will you practice trust in God as your provider?}

{red|How will you cultivate contentment amid financial difficulty?}

Write your stewardship commitment:

{red|**Begin to envision your long-term financial future.**

What do you want your financial life to look like in five years?}{red| What about in ten years or at retirement?}{red| What long-term financial goals matter most to you?}{red| What is one small step toward long-term health you could take now?}

Write your financial vision:

{red|What is the most important thing you have learned or realized through this journey about your finances?}

{red|What do you want to remember when financial stress rises?}

Write your key financial insight:

{red|What specific commitments will you make for your ongoing financial health?}

{red|How will you manage your budget?}{red| How will you continue addressing debt?}{red| How will you build savings?}{red| How will you handle financial anxiety when it arises?}

